{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi5R2f6awGLvnseVbGqc","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01EcA17QBpPWLpr6s7JCuxKs","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\np='report.json'; R=json.load(open(p))\nSI=R['sourceInformation']\nSI['testing']=(\"Allowed: headless scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine's game.advance and game.debug), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, kept behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit; file:// preferred, and a server only on 127.0.0.1 at an OS-picked port (for the AI Hub harness), stopping only my own processes by PID. \"\n  \"Ran: node tests/run.mjs, 209 headless checks in 8 suites (unit, every site of every hunt cleared by a bot, the full campaign from title to lodge, every mode, the hub client against a stub, attract, dev mode, saves, determinism); --browser, 21 Playwright/Chromium checks (14 deep links load clean, the pick-agreement check that what is drawn is what is hit, a touch-only phone, controller glyphs); --hub, the AI Hub harness's own ?auto=1 run at 2 players, at 4 players with 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss, and with the host leaving: all pass\")\nSI['environment']=\"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\"\nSI['online']=\"AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 players: the game's own ready card, a timed 3-minute Hub Hunt-Off keyed to the hub clock (late joiners catch up), ghost reticles and shots of the other hunters, progress each second and one result; it passes the AI Hub harness's automatic checks (2 players; 4 players on a bad network; the host leaving). Standalone without the hub\"\nSI['usage']=\"unknown\"\nR['testingFeedback']=[\n \"About 35 real game bugs that I logged and fixed, not counting a handful of bugs in the tests themselves and the lint's false alarms. Examples: the first-person lens was only set up when drawing, so headless visibility was wrong; the attract bot never hit running bucks; time-up bucks were not counted as escaped; the trophy Duke never heard calls while hidden; dialog boxes were never drawn during hunts; the camera sat at the map corner before the opening dialog; hub copies' spawns drifted apart; and five separate mismatches between what was drawn and what a shot hit (wading legs, hooves in grass, the renderer's rounded view angles, the rig's root height, overlapping body regions).\",\n \"6: a load-time crash (a constant used before it was defined), a crash on the trophy kill (a class used before it was defined), the Range, Bonus-only and Quick modes never ending (their segment queues had no final step), the Range's pump station could not be finished, the online copies' animals drifting apart (so players hunted different deer), and the story and tutorial dialog never being drawn.\",\n \"The pick-agreement browser check: it renders each animal through the real renderer, fires rays through the pixels it drew and 3 pixels outside them, and requires at least 95% hits inside and at most 5% outside. It found that what you saw was not what you could shoot in five different ways that no amount of playing at normal speed would have shown clearly. Close second: the scripted full playthrough, title to lodge, with a bot that has to clear every site.\",\n \"The banned-word lint in the build (TODO, placeholder and similar). It found no real stubs and three false alarms (a method named Glyph.prompt, a comment that mentioned Math.random, and a HUD state named 'todo'), which I renamed to keep it quiet.\"]\nR['engineFeedback']=[\n \"The fixed 1/120 s step and the fact that everything can be stepped: game.advance, game.debug, scripts with _step and seeded random streams. That made a headless whole-game playthrough, bots clearing every site, the hub's catch-up for late joiners and determinism checks easy. The Shell menus and settings, the procedural Humanoid rigs, the synth and the font also gave the game a consistent look and sound fast.\",\n \"Yes, a few times: game.saves is memory-only headless (I first assumed my save tests were broken); input presets must keep start, pause, confirm and cancel; the Shell's settings screen had no way to group or hide rows; I ran site scripts on the game clock, so hub copies drifted (fixed with a site clock); closing the pause menu also swept away my dev screen; and I assumed E.View was exact, but views are cached at rounded yaw, pitch and scale, so drawn animals were up to 7 px from their hit zones near the screen edges.\",\n \"Vista, a first-person pseudo-3D landscape renderer: a column-marched heightfield with occlusion, water, fog, mist, a canopy, crops, billboards hidden by hills, a magnified scope lens and an exact perspective projection that shots share. The engine is built around orthographic and side views and has nothing for a first-person landscape. Second: the procedural Quadruped rig (gaits with planted hooves, poses, a death roll, and hit zones from the same joints).\",\n \"Fitting a perspective first-person game into an engine whose views are orthographic. The animals are drawn with engine views, but shots go through a perspective lens, and keeping the two the same pixel for pixel took most of the debugging. The engine being one 12,000-line file made it slow to find things.\",\n \"The glyph setting now defaults to 'playstation', because the task asks for PlayStation glyphs by default. The Shell's settings screens skip hidden settings and accept a rows list with section headings, so settings could be grouped and internal ones hidden. ChipAudio.bus() defaults to the 'sfx' bus. I also added three modules: vista.js (the landscape renderer, including waterline clipping for wading animals and an exact projection for each billboard), quadruped.js (four-legged rigs and their hit zones, with a lookup that decides shoulder, gut or ham by where a round enters) and babble.js (gibberish voices).\",\n \"A first-class perspective camera (project, ray, billboards drawn with the same projection), so first-person and rail-shooter games get matching drawing and hit tests for free instead of building their own.\"]\nR['promptFeedback']=[\n \"It is concrete and checkable: the 10-foot UI rules, the glyph behaviour, attract mode after 10 s, the touch-only message, the dev-mode sliders and the exact exit-interview shape all turn straight into tests. The rules about shared ports and processes were clear too.\",\n \"Two field definitions. 'Testing: testing parameters allowed' could mean the rules I was given or what I actually ran, so I wrote both. 'Environment' comes pre-filled while the task also says to write unknown for anything unconfirmed. Also, PLAN.md was written for a different reasoning level, and its file names had to be swapped for TASK.md's, which STAGE.md did settle.\",\n \"Making a convincing arcade light-gun hunt in a 2D pixel engine: a first-person landscape you can see a quarter mile across, deer and elk that walk, graze, spook, run and fall believably, and shots that hit exactly what is drawn, all deterministic enough for online play and headless tests.\",\n \"Add a prioritised must-have list or a rough time budget for the 'feature-complete vertical slice', since the scope is open-ended. Also say whether the feedback in the chat reply has to match the About screen word for word (I made them identical).\"]\njson.dump(R, open(p,'w'), indent=2, ensure_ascii=False); open(p,'a').write('\\n')\nEOF\nnode build/build.mjs 2>&1 | tail -2; python3 -c \"import json; d=json.load(open('../exit-interview.json')); print(len(d['sourceInformation']), len(d['testingFeedback']), len(d['engineFeedback']), len(d['promptFeedback']), list(d['aiHub'].keys())); print([k for k,v in d['sourceInformation'].items() if not v])\"","description":"Fill report feedback, rebuild, verify exit interview","timeout":200000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2720,"cache_read_input_tokens":180546,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2720},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8fb56c77-99e2-4018-a816-6b0a42973b6b","uuid":"54f8440a-8167-417c-b4bf-f491fe6669a5","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:52:45.316Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi5R2JmbjqpEaJzH4jRQ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01EcA17QBpPWLpr6s7JCuxKs":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport json\np='report.json'; R=json.load(open(p))\nSI=R['sourceInformation']\nSI['testing']=(\"Allowed: headless scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine's game.advance and game.debug), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, kept behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit; file:// preferred, and a server only on 127.0.0.1 at an OS-picked port (for the AI Hub harness), stopping only my own processes by PID. \"\n  \"Ran: node tests/run.mjs, 209 headless checks in 8 suites (unit, every site of every hunt cleared by a bot, the full campaign from title to lodge, every mode, the hub client against a stub, attract, dev mode, saves, determinism); --browser, 21 Playwright/Chromium checks (14 deep links load clean, the pick-agreement check that what is drawn is what is hit, a touch-only phone, controller glyphs); --hub, the AI Hub harness's own ?auto=1 run at 2 players, at 4 players with 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss, and with the host leaving: all pass\")\nSI['environment']=\"Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\"\nSI['online']=\"AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 players: the game's own ready card, a timed 3-minute Hub Hunt-Off keyed to the hub clock (late joiners catch up), ghost reticles and shots of the other hunters, progress each second and one result; it passes the AI Hub harness's automatic checks (2 players; 4 players on a bad network; the host leaving). Standalone without the hub\"\nSI['usage']=\"unknown\"\nR['testingFeedback']=[\n \"About 35 real game bugs that I logged and fixed, not counting a handful of bugs in the tests themselves and the lint's false alarms. Examples: the first-person lens was only set up when drawing, so headless visibility was wrong; the attract bot never hit running bucks; time-up bucks were not counted as escaped; the trophy Duke never heard calls while hidden; dialog boxes were never drawn during hunts; the camera sat at the map corner before the opening dialog; hub copies' spawns drifted apart; and five separate mismatches between what was drawn and what a shot hit (wading legs, hooves in grass, the renderer's rounded view angles, the rig's root height, overlapping body regions).\",\n \"6: a load-time crash (a constant used before it was defined), a crash on the trophy kill (a class used before it was defined), the Range, Bonus-only and Quick modes never ending (their segment queues had no final step), the Range's pump station could not be finished, the online copies' animals drifting apart (so players hunted different deer), and the story and tutorial dialog never being drawn.\",\n \"The pick-agreement browser check: it renders each animal through the real renderer, fires rays through the pixels it drew and 3 pixels outside them, and requires at least 95% hits inside and at most 5% outside. It found that what you saw was not what you could shoot in five different ways that no amount of playing at normal speed would have shown clearly. Close second: the scripted full playthrough, title to lodge, with a bot that has to clear every site.\",\n \"The banned-word lint in the build (TODO, placeholder and similar). It found no real stubs and three false alarms (a method named Glyph.prompt, a comment that mentioned Math.random, and a HUD state named 'todo'), which I renamed to keep it quiet.\"]\nR['engineFeedback']=[\n \"The fixed 1/120 s step and the fact that everything can be stepped: game.advance, game.debug, scripts with _step and seeded random streams. That made a headless whole-game playthrough, bots clearing every site, the hub's catch-up for late joiners and determinism checks easy. The Shell menus and settings, the procedural Humanoid rigs, the synth and the font also gave the game a consistent look and sound fast.\",\n \"Yes, a few times: game.saves is memory-only headless (I first assumed my save tests were broken); input presets must keep start, pause, confirm and cancel; the Shell's settings screen had no way to group or hide rows; I ran site scripts on the game clock, so hub copies drifted (fixed with a site clock); closing the pause menu also swept away my dev screen; and I assumed E.View was exact, but views are cached at rounded yaw, pitch and scale, so drawn animals were up to 7 px from their hit zones near the screen edges.\",\n \"Vista, a first-person pseudo-3D landscape renderer: a column-marched heightfield with occlusion, water, fog, mist, a canopy, crops, billboards hidden by hills, a magnified scope lens and an exact perspective projection that shots share. The engine is built around orthographic and side views and has nothing for a first-person landscape. Second: the procedural Quadruped rig (gaits with planted hooves, poses, a death roll, and hit zones from the same joints).\",\n \"Fitting a perspective first-person game into an engine whose views are orthographic. The animals are drawn with engine views, but shots go through a perspective lens, and keeping the two the same pixel for pixel took most of the debugging. The engine being one 12,000-line file made it slow to find things.\",\n \"The glyph setting now defaults to 'playstation', because the task asks for PlayStation glyphs by default. The Shell's settings screens skip hidden settings and accept a rows list with section headings, so settings could be grouped and internal ones hidden. ChipAudio.bus() defaults to the 'sfx' bus. I also added three modules: vista.js (the landscape renderer, including waterline clipping for wading animals and an exact projection for each billboard), quadruped.js (four-legged rigs and their hit zones, with a lookup that decides shoulder, gut or ham by where a round enters) and babble.js (gibberish voices).\",\n \"A first-class perspective camera (project, ray, billboards drawn with the same projection), so first-person and rail-shooter games get matching drawing and hit tests for free instead of building their own.\"]\nR['promptFeedback']=[\n \"It is concrete and checkable: the 10-foot UI rules, the glyph behaviour, attract mode after 10 s, the touch-only message, the dev-mode sliders and the exact exit-interview shape all turn straight into tests. The rules about shared ports and processes were clear too.\",\n \"Two field definitions. 'Testing: testing parameters allowed' could mean the rules I was given or what I actually ran, so I wrote both. 'Environment' comes pre-filled while the task also says to write unknown for anything unconfirmed. Also, PLAN.md was written for a different reasoning level, and its file names had to be swapped for TASK.md's, which STAGE.md did settle.\",\n \"Making a convincing arcade light-gun hunt in a 2D pixel engine: a first-person landscape you can see a quarter mile across, deer and elk that walk, graze, spook, run and fall believably, and shots that hit exactly what is drawn, all deterministic enough for online play and headless tests.\",\n \"Add a prioritised must-have list or a rough time budget for the 'feature-complete vertical slice',