{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017pHkSQnvzfSpkMmEZ3jMRE","type":"tool_result","content":"built BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050516.html (1753 KB)\nThe engine's native retro-modern pixel style, at least 480x270 pixels and growing with the window (E.tones shading, 1-pixel outlines, the 5x7 font, E.font.title logos), with new procedural animals, antlers, trees, corn, reeds and farm buildings painted in code, and core-library furniture","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8fb56c77-99e2-4018-a816-6b0a42973b6b","uuid":"9275c432-9d87-4e7f-9f0c-9507a3e484a9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:59:52.297Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"built BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050516.html (1753 KB)\nThe engine's native retro-modern pixel style, at least 480x270 pixels and growing with the window (E.tones shading, 1-pixel outlines, the 5x7 font, E.font.title logos), with new procedural animals, antlers, trees, corn, reeds and farm buildings painted in code, and core-library furniture","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_017pHkSQnvzfSpkMmEZ3jMRE","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi61GwY4oQQDwmCrwuav","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Big Buck Hunt-Off is finished and every test passes: `node tests/run.mjs --browser --hub` gives **235 passed, 0 failed**. That is 209 headless checks, 22 browser checks, and 4 AI Hub checks: confirming the served page is the game, plus the hub's own automatic run with 2 players, with 4 players at 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss, and with the host leaving. The only changes after that run were wording in the report and in one engine comment, which no test covers.\n\n**Files**, in this folder's root:\n- `BigBuckHuntOff-BigBuckHunterPro-claude-opus-5-5-xhigh-20261104-050516.html`, the single bundled file (1.75 MB).\n- `exit-interview.json`, which parses as JSON with every field filled.\n\nA scan of both files finds no to-do markers, stand-in text or stubs. Dev files are in the subfolder of the same name.\n\n**Fixed in this last stretch:**\n- **Shots now hit what is drawn.** The browser check fires rays through each drawn animal; it now passes, with at least 95% of rays inside the drawing hitting and at most 5% just outside it hitting. Five separate mismatches caused the misses:\n  - Wading animals had their legs drawn over the water; the water now hides them.\n  - Ground just in front of the hooves blocked shots that the drawing showed as hits.\n  - Animals were drawn from a rounded-off camera angle, up to 7 px from their hit zones.\n  - Drawings were placed slightly below the animal's real body position.\n  - A rump shot could score as gut.\n- **Menu:** long labels no longer run off their tiles.\n- **Lodge:** the camera stays inside the room and the lamp glows are smaller.\n- **About pages:** text now fills each page by measured lines instead of running off the panel.\n\nSource Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 05:05:16\n- **Source:** Big Buck Hunter Pro (arcade, 2006; designed by Play Mechanix, made and distributed by Raw Thrills)\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre:** Arcade light-gun hunting shooter (a shooting gallery in a living landscape) with party versus and bonus mini-games\n- **Perspective:** First person on rails: a fixed vantage per site with scripted camera pans over a pseudo-3D heightfield landscape; a slow-motion Trophy Cam on big kills; side-on x-ray Shot Charts; a side-view Lodge; 2D menus\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package (full engine, commit 4390a98) with new engine modules vista.js, quadruped.js and babble.js; vanilla JavaScript, Canvas 2D, Web Audio; AI Hub session client 1.4.0 (ai-hub f8835e4); Node 24 headless tests; Playwright 1.63.0 with Chromium 1243\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native retro-modern pixel style, at least 480x270 pixels and growing with the window (E.tones shading, 1-pixel outlines, the 5x7 font, E.font.title logos), with new procedural animals, antlers, trees, corn, reeds and farm buildings painted in code, and core-library furniture\n- **Animation:** The engine's native procedural rigs posed by math every step: E.Humanoid for the cast, E.Blob for the hound, and new procedural E.Quadruped (deer, elk, moose, canids, the cow, small critters) and E.Bird rigs with planted-hoof gaits, poses and a death roll; engine tweens and scripts for cameras and UI\n- **Audio:** All synthesized in code: bluegrass chiptune songs on the engine synth (banjo-roll pulse, square fiddle, triangle bass, noise washboard) with layers and stings, layered rifle shots with valley echoes, calls, animal voices, ambience beds, and Animalese-style gibberish voices (E.Babble)\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 players: the game's own ready card, a timed 3-minute Hub Hunt-Off keyed to the hub clock (late joiners catch up), ghost reticles and shots of the other hunters, progress each second and one result; it passes the AI Hub harness's automatic checks (2 players; 4 players on a bad network; the host leaving). Standalone without the hub\n- **Testing:** Allowed: headless scripts that play the game and check its state (the engine's game.advance and game.debug), including a scripted playthrough of the whole game, kept behind one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit; file:// preferred, and a server only on 127.0.0.1 at an OS-picked port (for the AI Hub harness), stopping only my own processes by PID. Ran: node tests/run.mjs, 209 headless checks in 8 suites (unit, every site of every hunt cleared by a bot, the full campaign from title to lodge, every mode, the hub client against a simulated hub, attract, dev mode, saves, determinism); --browser, 22 Playwright/Chromium checks (14 deep links load clean, the pick-agreement check that what is drawn is what is hit, a touch-only phone, controller glyphs); --hub, the AI Hub harness's own ?auto=1 run at 2 players, at 4 players with 150 ms latency, 50 ms jitter and 5% loss, and with the host leaving: all pass\n- **Research:** Planning stage (2026-11-03): Wikipedia 'Big Buck Hunter', the Highway Games listing and Macworld's iPhone review read directly; Giant Bomb, TV Tropes, MobyGames and the Sinden wiki only as search summaries; the my-3D2dge handoff docs and the AI Hub protocol docs. Build stage: no web research; the engine's and the hub's own docs and source\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort xhigh\n- **Usage:** unknown\n\nTesting Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 35 real game bugs that I logged and fixed, not counting a handful of bugs in the tests themselves and the lint's false alarms. Examples: the first-person lens was only set up when drawing, so headless visibility was wrong; the attract bot never hit running bucks; time-up bucks were not counted as escaped; the trophy Duke never heard calls while hidden; dialog boxes were never drawn during hunts; the camera sat at the map corner before the opening dialog; hub copies' spawns drifted apart; and five separate mismatches between what was drawn and what a shot hit (wading legs, hooves in grass, the renderer's rounded view angles, the rig's root height, overlapping body regions).\n2. **How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?** 6: a load-time crash (a constant used before it was defined), a crash on the trophy kill (a class used before it was defined), the Range, Bonus-only and Quick modes never ending (their segment queues had no final step), the Range's pump station could not be finished, the online copies' animals drifting apart (so players hunted different deer), and the story and tutorial dialog never being drawn.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The pick-agreement browser check: it renders each animal through the real renderer, fires rays through the pixels it drew and 3 pixels outside them, and requires at least 95% hits inside and at most 5% outside. It found that what you saw was not what you could shoot in five different ways that no amount of playing at normal speed would have shown clearly. Close second: the scripted full playthrough, title to lodge, with a bot that has to clear every site.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The build's banned-word lint (it scans the source for leftover to-do markers and stand-in text). It found no real stubs and three false alarms (a method named Glyph.prompt, a comment that mentioned Math.random, and a HUD state whose name read as a to-do marker), which I renamed to keep it quiet.\n\nEngine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** The fixed 1/120 s step and the fact that everything can be stepped: game.advance, game.debug, scripts with _step and seeded random streams. That made a headless whole-game playthrough, bots clearing every site, the hub's catch-up for late joiners and determinism checks easy. The Shell menus and settings, the procedural Humanoid rigs, the synth and the font also gave the game a consistent look and sound fast.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, a few times: game.saves is memory-only headless (I first assumed my save tests were broken); input presets must keep start, pause, confirm and cancel; the Shell's settings screen had no way to group or hide rows; I ran site scripts on the game clock, so hub copies drifted (fixed with a site clock); closing the pause menu also swept away my dev screen; and I assumed E.View was exact, but views are cached at rounded yaw, pitch and scale, so drawn animals were up to 7 px from their hit zones near the screen edges.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** Vista, a first-person pseudo-3D landscape renderer: a column-marched heightfield with occlusion, water, fog, mist, a canopy, crops, billboards hidden by hills, a magnified scope lens and an exact perspective projection that shots share. The engine is built around orthographic and side views and has nothing for a first-person landscape. Second: the procedural Quadruped rig (gaits with planted hooves, poses, a death roll, and hit zones from the same joints).\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting a perspective first-person game into an engine whose views are orthographic. The animals are drawn with engine views, but shots go through a perspective lens, and keeping the two the same pixel for pixel took most of the debugging. The engine being one file of nearly 15,000 lines made it slow to find things.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** The glyph setting now defaults to 'playstation', because the task asks for PlayStation glyphs by default. The Shell's settings screens skip hidden settings and accept a rows list with section headings, so settings could be grouped and internal ones hidden. ChipAudio.bus() defaults to the 'sfx' bus. I also added three modules: vista.js (the landscape renderer, including waterline clipping for wading animals and an exact projection for each billboard), quadruped.js (four-legged rigs and their hit zones, with a lookup that decides shoulder, gut or ham by where a round enters) and babble.js (gibberish voices).\n6. **If you could change one thing?** A first-class perspective camera (project, ray, billboards drawn with the same projection), so first-person and rail-shooter games get matching drawing and hit tests for free instead of building their own.\n\nPrompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** It is concrete and checkable: the 10-foot UI rules, the glyph behaviour, attract mode after 10 s, the touch-only message, the dev-mode sliders and the exact exit-interview shape all turn straight into tests. The rules about shared ports and processes were clear too.\n2. **What was confusing?** Two field definitions. 'Testing: testing parameters allowed' could mean the rules I was given or what I actually ran, so I wrote both. 'Environment' comes pre-filled while the task also says to write unknown for anything unconfirmed. Also, PLAN.md was written for a different reasoning level, and its file names had to be swapped for TASK.md's, which STAGE.md did settle.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Making a convincing arcade light-gun hunt in a 2D pixel engine: a first-person landscape you can see a quarter mile across, deer and elk that walk, graze, spook, run and fall believably, and shots that hit exactly what is drawn, all deterministic enough for online play and headless tests.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Add a prioritised must-have list or a rough time budget for the 'feature-complete vertical slice', since the scope is open-ended. Also say whether the feedback in the chat reply has to match the About screen word for word (I made them identical)."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":473,"cache_read_input_tokens":209000,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":473},"output_tokens":772,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"8fb56c77-99e2-4018-a816-6b0a42973b6b","uuid":"3216331e-1629-42be-b7ff-04905b162b55","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:00:22.439Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi61GdRjUtK7CdXdJvin"}
